NAME
  mkinstaller - make an installer floppy that sets up a computer with no OS

SYNOPSIS
  mkinstaller DEST [PROJECT...] [--os[=GROUP,...]] [--skip=PATH,...] [--label=TEXT]
              [--name=PATTERN [--first=N]] [--auto=SECONDS]

DESCRIPTION
  Writes an installer to the floppy mounted at DEST. A computer with that floppy and an empty
  hard drive boots it from the BIOS, installs the chosen packages, makes the hard drive its
  boot drive and reboots into it.

  Each PROJECT is a folder with a bundle.cfg, as for bundle pack. Two extra keys say what the
  installer does at first boot:
    {name="mill", paths={"etc","usr"}, enable={"worker"}, hostname=true}
  enable lists rc daemons to enable; hostname=true asks for a hostname.

  A hands-off install beeps twice when done and waits for the floppy to come out, then reboots.
  A computer with no screen or keyboard always installs hands-off.
  If the floppy runs out of room, mkinstaller says so and removes what it wrote.

OPTIONS
  --os
    Also pack this OS in osinstall's groups: required, qos, sys, basic, man, unsorted. /home,
    /tmp, /mnt and machine state (hostname, tnet.hosts, rc.cfg, filesystem.cfg, .qboot) are
    never packed, and neither is any file in a PROJECT's paths (excluded ones too).

  --os=GROUP,...
    Pack only those groups. Leaving out required is allowed, with a warning: such a floppy
    can't make an empty drive bootable.

  --skip=PATH,...
    Leave these files or folders out of the OS.

  --label=TEXT
    The label given to a drive that has none; also names the installer.

  --name=PATTERN
    Name each computer from a pattern with one number, e.g. mill-%02d. A writable floppy
    counts up after each install; a read-only one asks for a name instead.

  --first=N
    The first number for --name (default 1).

  --auto=SECONDS
    Install the ticked packages after that many seconds unless a key is pressed.
